# The ledger of `html/sanitize`: what the HTML sanitizers of the three
# languages let a program do, one block per capability, and what Rebel
# answers. None of the three standard libraries has one, so the yardstick is
# the author's of 2026-11-17: Go's `github.com/microcosm-cc/bluemonday`
# v1.0.27 (2,859 importers), Rust's `ammonia` 4.2.5, Python's `bleach` 6.4.0
# and `nh3` 0.3.8 (ammonia's binding), and the WHATWG HTML standard's
# Sanitizer API as the measure of what a sanitizer must hold, named in the
# notes. The lists are this machine's, 2026-11-17: `go doc -all` and
# `go doc -all -u` of bluemonday fetched into the scratch folder's module
# cache, `dir()` and the signatures of bleach and nh3 installed in a virtual
# environment of the scratch folder, the item list and the `Builder` methods
# docs.rs prints for ammonia 4.2.5, and the standard's section 8.6, "HTML
# sanitization", fetched from html.spec.whatwg.org (dynamic-markup-insertion
# and the pages of each element's sanitization category).
#
# The expected answers of the tests were observed: bluemonday's by a Go
# program of the scratch folder, nh3's and bleach's by Python there. The
# elements and attributes of `ugc_policy` are bluemonday's `UGCPolicy` as it
# answered 144 element names and 160 attribute names with twelve values each;
# its value checks are this package's own, the ones bluemonday exports named
# as it names them. The XSS vectors of OWASP's cheat sheet (395 inputs, the
# module `html/sanitize/internal/owasp`) were cleaned by the three ready
# policies, and every answer was read by Go's `golang.org/x/net/html`, which
# found nothing a policy refuses and wrote back the same tree, its escapes
# aside; the answers of
# the policy for user-generated content agree with nh3's, given the same
# lists, on 391 of the 395, the four others noted below.

capability sanitize/clean  a fragment from outside cleaned by a policy and written as text
  heroes  html/sanitize.clean
  go      Policy.Sanitize, Policy.SanitizeBytes
  rust    clean, Builder::clean, Document, Document::to_string, Document::write_to
  python  bleach.clean, bleach.Cleaner, Cleaner.clean, nh3.clean, nh3.Cleaner, nh3.Cleaner.clean, html5lib filters.sanitizer
  note    read as a browser reads it, by `html` (the WHATWG tree construction), as ammonia and html5lib read it; bluemonday filters the tokens of a text and builds no tree, so its answer keeps a tag unclosed where the input did (observed: `<b>unclosed <i>nest</b> after` stays so, here `<b>unclosed <i>nest</i></b><i> after</i>`) and writes a table with no `tbody`. Bytes are text once read as UTF-8, so `SanitizeBytes` is `clean` after the language's conversion

capability sanitize/settled  the answer read again and cleaned again until a reading takes nothing out (mutation XSS)
  heroes  html/sanitize.clean
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    none of the three reads its answer again; the standard's 8.6.6.4 names mutation XSS and its own Sanitizer works on the tree it parses, never on a text. Here the answer is read inside the context element and cleaned again, at most eight times, until a reading takes nothing out and writes the same bytes; so `clean(p, clean(p, x)) == clean(p, x)`, held by a test for every ready policy and every OWASP vector. Where eight readings do not settle, the answer is the text alone, escaped

capability sanitize/tree  the cleaned fragment as a tree a program walks
  heroes  html/sanitize.clean_fragment
  go      -
  rust    Builder::clean (its `Document`)
  python  -
  note    the tree of the settled answer, read as a browser reads it; the standard's `setHTML` and `parseHTML` answer a tree too

capability sanitize/stream  a stream cleaned as it is read
  heroes  refused: the tree is built from the whole text and the answer is read again before it is given, so nothing can be written before the last byte is read; a stream is read whole by `io` first
  go      Policy.SanitizeReader, Policy.SanitizeReaderToWriter
  rust    Builder::clean_from_reader
  python  -

capability sanitize/policy  a policy held as a value, made and changed
  heroes  html/sanitize.Policy, html/sanitize.strict_policy
  go      Policy, NewPolicy
  rust    Builder, Builder::new, Builder::empty
  python  bleach.Cleaner, nh3.Cleaner
  note    a value, copied like any other, changed by functions that take it with `@`; the standard's `Sanitizer` and its `SanitizerConfig`. `strict_policy` is the empty policy every other starts from, as bluemonday's `NewPolicy`

capability sanitize/strict  a ready policy that keeps the text alone
  heroes  html/sanitize.strict_policy
  go      StrictPolicy, StripTagsPolicy
  rust    Builder::empty
  python  -
  note    as bluemonday's: every element unwrapped but frame, frameset, iframe, noembed, noframes, noscript, object, script, style and title, which go with their content (observed); an SVG or MathML element goes with its content too, where bluemonday keeps the text of both (observed `xafter` and `tafter`)

capability sanitize/ugc  a ready policy for user-generated content
  heroes  html/sanitize.ugc_policy
  go      UGCPolicy
  rust    Builder::default
  python  nh3.ALLOWED_TAGS, nh3.ALLOWED_ATTRIBUTES, nh3.ALLOWED_URL_SCHEMES, nh3.CLEAN_CONTENT_TAGS, bleach.ALLOWED_TAGS, bleach.ALLOWED_ATTRIBUTES, bleach.ALLOWED_PROTOCOLS
  note    bluemonday's elements and attributes, observed, with `nofollow` on every link. Where they differ, decided: an `a` or `img` left with no attribute stays, as ammonia keeps it, where bluemonday takes it out; the `cite` of `del` and `ins` is checked as the URL it is, where bluemonday checks it as text (observed `x` kept, `http://e.com/a` refused); `rel` is added only to a link that has an `href`, where nh3 adds it to every `a`; a relative URL that `url` cannot read (`java\0script:...`, a colon in its first segment) is refused, where nh3 keeps it. nh3's default, ammonia's (75 elements, 25 schemes, `noopener noreferrer`) and bleach's (12 elements) are their own lists, not offered: a program builds either from `strict_policy`

capability sanitize/rich-text  a ready policy for rich text, the standard's default
  heroes  html/sanitize.rich_text_policy
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    the standard's built-in safe default configuration (8.6.5): its HTML elements of the category Default with their attributes, its MathML and SVG table, its global attributes. Its URLs are `http`, `https`, `mailto` and relative ones, where the standard's keeps every scheme but `javascript`: a `data:` or `blob:` link is refused here

capability sanitize/elements  elements allowed by name
  heroes  html/sanitize.allow_elements
  go      Policy.AllowElements, Policy.AllowNoAttrs, attrPolicyBuilder.AllowNoAttrs
  rust    Builder::tags, Builder::add_tags
  python  bleach.clean(tags=), nh3.clean(tags=)
  note    an SVG element `svg:name`, a MathML one `math:name`, as the standard's `SanitizerElementNamespace` names them; an element allowed stays with no attribute, so bluemonday's `AllowNoAttrs` has nothing to do. The standard's `allowElement`

capability sanitize/elements-matching  elements allowed by a pattern over their names
  heroes  refused: an element is named or not; a pattern lets in elements nobody named, custom elements a page's scripts may act on (the standard's 8.6.6.3, script gadgets)
  go      Policy.AllowElementsMatching, attrPolicyBuilder.OnElementsMatching, stylePolicyBuilder.OnElementsMatching
  rust    -
  python  -

capability sanitize/drop  an element taken out with everything inside it
  heroes  html/sanitize.remove_elements, html/sanitize.Removal
  go      Policy.SkipElementsContent
  rust    Builder::clean_content_tags, Builder::add_clean_content_tags, Builder::rm_clean_content_tags
  python  nh3.clean(clean_content_tags=), nh3.CLEAN_CONTENT_TAGS
  note    the standard's `removeElement`. An SVG or MathML element not allowed always goes with its content: the standard's non-replaceable list keeps `svg` and `math` from being unwrapped, since what they hold is read by other rules once their tags are gone

capability sanitize/unwrap  an element taken out and its content kept
  heroes  html/sanitize.remove_elements, html/sanitize.set_unlisted
  go      Policy.AllowElementsContent
  rust    Builder::rm_tags
  python  bleach.clean(strip=True)
  note    what becomes of an element no list names is the policy's: unwrapped in the ready ones, as ammonia and nh3 do; the standard's `replaceElementWithChildren`

capability sanitize/escape  an element not allowed written as text
  heroes  html/sanitize.remove_elements, html/sanitize.set_unlisted
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  bleach.clean(strip=False)
  note    bleach's default (observed `<u>y</u>` written `&lt;u&gt;y&lt;/u&gt;`). bleach shows the tag as the input wrote it; the tree keeps no source, so the tag is written again, each value between `"` (`id=y` is shown `id="y"`)

capability sanitize/unsafe  elements that run a script kept where a policy asks
  heroes  refused: a policy that can keep a script is no sanitizer; `html` reads and writes such markup. The standard's safe baseline (base, embed, frame, iframe, object, script, SVG script and use) is never kept, nor `style` and SVG `style`, whose sheet reaches the whole page, nor `plaintext`, which would make the rest of a page text, nor an event handler or `srcdoc`
  go      Policy.AllowUnsafe
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    the standard's `removeUnsafe` and its safe methods, applied whatever the policy says; bluemonday refuses `script` and `style` unless `AllowUnsafe`. Naming one of them in `allow_elements` changes nothing, as the face says

capability sanitize/iframes  inline frames kept with a sandbox
  heroes  refused: `iframe` is in the standard's safe baseline: it loads a page of its own, and its `srcdoc` is HTML
  go      Policy.AllowIFrames, Policy.RequireSandboxOnIFrame, SandboxValue, SandboxAllowDownloads, SandboxAllowDownloadsWithoutUserActivation, SandboxAllowForms, SandboxAllowModals, SandboxAllowOrientationLock, SandboxAllowPointerLock, SandboxAllowPopups, SandboxAllowPopupsToEscapeSandbox, SandboxAllowPresentation, SandboxAllowSameOrigin, SandboxAllowScripts, SandboxAllowStorageAccessByUserActivation, SandboxAllowTopNavigation, SandboxAllowTopNavigationByUserActivation
  rust    -
  python  -

